<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Memory. Both keys are supported on visionOS, neither needs Apple's approval
  (Xcode's own capability database: supportedSDKs includes VISION_OS,
  distributionApprovalRequired false), and automatic signing adds them to the
  App ID on the next build with -allowProvisioningUpdates.

  We want them because this app is not shaped like a normal one: it hosts a
  whole other runtime. Measured on device at three swapchain stages, the
  footprint is ~3.5 GB — IL2CPP's heap, Unity's assets and textures, ANGLE, and
  ~490 MB of eye textures, with a transient spike at every loading transition
  where two generations of the eye swapchain are briefly live at once.

  increased-memory-limit
      Raises the jetsam limit — how much the app may use before the OS kills it.
      This is the one that matters for the transition spike.

  extended-virtual-addressing
      Raises the limit on address SPACE rather than resident memory. A separate
      ceiling, and one this design leans on independently of footprint: the
      guest ELF images are mmapped, the veneer pool and stub cells are mapped,
      ANGLE maps its own, and the synthetic /proc tree is materialised on disk
      and mapped too. Address space is cheap to consume and expensive to
      discover you have run out of.

  NOT increased-debugging-memory-limit: iOS only, no VISION_OS in its
  supportedSDKs, and an entitlement the platform does not know is a
  provisioning failure rather than a no-op.

  ===========================================================================
  And NOT com.apple.developer.networking.multicast, which was here and is gone.

  Steam Link discovers hosts by UDP BROADCAST to port 27036 (SL-5 measured it),
  and visionOS gates broadcast behind that entitlement — which Apple grants by
  REQUEST rather than by ticking a capability box. That makes it categorically
  different from the two above: they are self-service, it is a wait, and until
  it is granted attaching it fails the BUILD and takes the memory pair with it.

  It is not needed. kl_shim.c delivers the guest's broadcast as unicast to each
  host on the subnet instead (`make bcast`), which needs only the free
  local-network permission — Info.plist's NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription — and
  that is what found the PC on a physical Vision Pro. Do not put it back
  without a measurement saying the fan-out is insufficient; the entitlement is
  also genuinely broad, since it lets the app send to and receive from every
  device on the network, and the fan-out's reach is one subnet sweep.

  ===========================================================================
  low-latency-streaming, which is in the third category: WANTED, and NOT
  self-service.

  This app is a real-time video client — HEVC off the wire, decoded on
  VideoToolbox, presented at display rate in an immersive space — which is the
  workload the key names. Added at the user's request.

  What is known and what is not, stated plainly so the next session does not
  re-derive it: the key does not appear anywhere in the XROS SDK or Xcode's
  Library tree. That is NOT evidence against it — neither of the two memory
  keys above appears there either, and those demonstrably work, so Xcode's
  capability database simply is not a greppable file. It is listed here as
  unverified rather than as confirmed.

  Because it is unverified it is FILTERABLE: gen_xcodeproj.py drops it when
  KLEPTON_LOW_LATENCY=0, writing a generated copy. That exists for exactly the
  failure the multicast section above describes — attaching a key the App ID
  has not been granted, or that the platform does not recognise, fails the
  whole signing step and takes the two memory keys with it. One knob, and the
  memory keys survive.

  It is also not a diagnosis. It was added while chasing a streaming
  regression, and the change that actually altered what the client publishes to
  the host is the interaction profile now being answered — KL_XR_PROFILE=0 in
  runtime/kl_openxr.c is that A/B. Settle the cause there before concluding
  anything about this key.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.developer.low-latency-streaming</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
